            "text": "Every morning begins with a negotiation between light and shadow. Before the streets fill, windows brighten one by one, as if the buildings are remembering themselves. A kettle clicks in a quiet kitchen, and that small sound feels strangely official: the day has been approved.\n\nOutside, a delivery cyclist coasts past, jacket bright as a kingfisher. A dog lifts its head from a doorway, considers the world, then returns to sleep. Somewhere, a bus exhales at the curb, releasing people who carry their private weather: worries like clouds, hopes like sudden blue.\n\nI have always liked this hour because nothing has fully happened yet. Mistakes remain reversible. Conversations wait behind unopened doors. Even the future seems less demanding when it is still only a possibility.\n\nMaybe that is why small rituals matter. Washing a cup. Folding a blanket. Watering the stubborn plant beside the window. These acts do not solve anything, but they place a hand gently on the shoulder of time and say, \u201cBegin here.\u201d\n\nBy noon, the world will be louder and more complicated. Messages will arrive with their sharp little demands. Plans will shift. Someone will misunderstand someone else. Yet beneath all that noise, the morning will continue glowing in memory, a quiet room we can revisit.\n\nPerhaps peace is not a permanent condition. Perhaps it is a practice of noticing: the mug, light, mercy of an unhurried breath. To notice these things is to refuse the idea that life is only what demands our attention."
          }
        ],
